1: interaction forces in dynamic atomic force microscopy,” APPL. PHYS.\nL802: \nL803: LETT., vol. 91, pp. 123106, 2007.\nL804: \nL805: [12] J. Legleiter, “The effect of drive frequency and set point amplitude on\nL806: \nL807: tapping forces in atomic force microscopy: simulation and exper iment,”\nL808: \nL809: Nanotechnology, vol. 20, pp. 245703. 2009.\nL810: \nL811: [13] FL. Leite, et al., “Theoretical Models for Surface Forces and Adhesion\nL812: \nL813: and Their Measurement Using Atomic Force Microscopy,” Int. J. Mol.\nL814: \nL815: Sci., vol. 13, pp. 12773-12856, 2012.\nL816: \nL817: 687\nL818: ## Citations (7)\nL819: ## References (15)\nL820: \nL821: ... Compression elements of genome resist pushing, while tension elements resist pulling. Table 3 provides all the necessary information about the Young's moduli of genomes [130,131,132,133,134,135,136,137,138,139,140]. The freely jointed chain model and the worm-like chain model developed in the theory of entropic elasticity of genomes could not be applied to reproduce such data using different methods of measurement [141]. ...\nL1014: Determining the peak interaction force between an oscillating nanoscale tip and a sample surface has been a fundamental yet elusive goal in amplitude-modulated atomic force microscopy. Closed form analytical expressions are derived using nonlinear asymptotic theory for the peak attractive and repulsive forces that approximate with a high degree of accuracy the numerically simulated peak forces under ambient or vacuum conditions.\nL1015: Scaling laws involving van der Waals, chemical forces, nanoscale elasticity, and oscillator parameters are identified to demonstrate approximate similitude for the peak interaction forces under practical operating conditions.\nL1016: 【45†View】\nL1017: \nL1018: Show abstract\nL1019: \nL1020: 【48†Scanning Force Microscopy】\nL1021: \nL1022: Chapter\nL1023: \nL1024:   * Jun 2008\nL1025: \nL1026:   * 【49†U.D. Schwarz】\nL1027: \nL1028: 【48†View】\nL1029: \nL1030: 【50†AuNPs conjugate DNA origami nanotubes for nanophotonic application】\nL1031: \nL1032: Conference Paper\nL1033: \nL1034:   * Mar 2012\nL1035: \nL1036:   * 【51†Sebastian Kiss】\nL1037:   * 【52†D. S. Hautzinger】\nL1038:   * 【53†Osamu Tabata】\nL1039:   * 【54†Jan Gerrit Korvink】\nL1040: \nL1041: 【50†View】\nL1042: \nL1043: 【55†DNA origami assembly on patterned silicon by AFM based lithography】\nL1044: \nL1045: Conference Paper\nL1046: \nL1047:   * Jan 2013\nL1048:   * 【56†T. Akishiba】\nL1049:   * 【57†N. Tamura】\nL1050:   * 【58†Takashi Ichii】\nL1051:   * 【53†Osamu Tabata】\nL1052: A new integration technique of Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) origami as an excellent platform to arrange various nanomaterials such as metallic nanoparticles, carbon nanotubes and proteins and so on with nanometer scale resolution into MEMS utilizing DNA hybridization of a complementary pair of single-strand DNAs (ssDNAs) was demonstrated.\nL1053: A contact-mode atomic force microscopy (AFM) based lithography was adopted to form ssDNA modified patterns into an organic thin film with 30 nm resolution on a silicon substrate. A newly designed DNA origami (30 nm × 150 nm) with ssDNA as sticky ends was successfully fixed at specific positions of the substrate by DNA hybridization.\nL1054: 【55†View】\nL1055: \nL1056: Show abstract\nL1057: \nL1058: 【59†Enhanced effects with scanning force microscopy】\nL1059: \nL1060: Article\nL1061: \nL1062:   * May 1991\nL1063: \nL1064:   * 【60†S. Howells】\nL1065:   * 【61†T. Chen】\nL1066:   * 【62†Mark J. Gallagher】\nL1067:   * 【63†Dror Sarid】\nL1068: We present a general theory that describes the operation of scanning force microscopy in the contact force regime. We find that force derivatives along the surface of a sample produce images that can be dramatically enhanced relative to those of surface topography. For scanning tunneling microscopy atomic force microscopy (STM/AFM) and AFM configurations, the spring constant of the cantilever and the force derivatives perpendicular to the surface of the sample determine the enhancement, repectively.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Thermal stability of DNA origami on mica (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/262604812_Thermal_stability_of_DNA_origami_on_mica)\nciteturn85view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn84view1\",\"pattern\":\"Rothemund\"}); Total lines: 1229\nL631: University of Notre Dame Materials Characterization\nL632: \nL633: Facility. The authors acknowledge support of the National\nL634: \nL635: Science Foundation through MRI award 1126374 for the\nL636: \nL637: XPS data in this paper.\nL638: \nL639: 1\nL640: \nL641: S. C. Deshmukh and E. S. Aydil, Appl. Phys. Lett. 65, 3185 (1994).\nL642: \nL643: 2\nL644: \nL645: H. T. Maune, S. Han, R. D. Barish, M. Bockrath, W. A. Goddard III, P.\nL646: \nL647: W. K. Rothemund, and E. Winfree, Nature Nanotechnol. 5, 61 (2010).\nL648: \nL649: 3\nL650: \nL651: R. Owczarzy, Y. You, B. G. Moreira, J. A. Manthey, L. Huang, M. A.\nL652: Behlke, and J. A. Walder, Biochemistry 43, 3537 (2004).\nL653: \nL654: 4\nL655: \nL656: Integrate DNA Technologies, Inc., OligoAnalyzer, http://www.idtdna.\nL657: \nL658: com/analyzer/applications/oligoanalyzer/ (accessed January 2014).\nL659: \nL660: 5\nL661: \nL662: The Nanobiotechnology Handbook, edited by Y. Xie (CRC Press/Taylor\nL663: \nL664: & Francis, Boca Raton, FL, 2013).\nL665: \nL666: 6\nL667: \nL668: J. Song et al., J. Am. Chem. Soc. 134, 9844 (2012).\nL669: \nL670: 7\nL671: \nL672: X. Wei, J. Nangreave, S. Jiang, H. Yan, and Y. Liu, J. Am. Chem. Soc.\nL673: \nL674: 135, 6165 (2013).\nL675: \nL676: 8\nL677: A. Rajendran, M. Endo, Y. Katsuda, K. Hidaka, and H. Sugiyama, J. Am.\nL678: \nL679: Chem. Soc. 133, 14488 (2011).\nL680: \nL681: 9\nL682: \nL683: P. W. K. Rothemund, Nature 440, 297 (2006).\nL684: \nL685: 10\nL686: \nL687: T. L. Sobey, S. Renner, and F. C. Simmel